    "sourceSha256": "4f76aac80a4260e1b4f867a508b774d04997aa3a47aa77fae0fa49c3d728669b",
    "note": "Three deposited states of Bacillus PS3 ATP synthase from one cryo-EM dataset: 6N2Y (3.0 \u00c5), 6N2Z (3.0 \u00c5) and 6N30 (3.2 \u00c5) (Guo, Suzuki and Rubinstein, eLife 2019). The coordinates are the prepared composite atp-synthase-figure, built from its recipe: the flipbook superposition of states 2 and 3 on state 1 by the stator, then one more rigid turn that puts the rotor axis along the line of sight, head toward the viewer, and one shift per state that sets the three states 140 Angstrom apart. Each state keeps its three beta subunits, gamma and epsilon as N, CA, C, O and CB atoms, plus Mg-ADP and putative phosphate. The alpha subunits, delta, the peripheral stalk, subunit a and the c-ring are left out. Mg-ADP marks the closed site; putative phosphate marks one open site; the other is empty. Fitted gamma orientations differ by 119.4 degrees from state 1 to 2 and 121.2 degrees from state 2 to 3, counterclockwise seen from the head."
  },
  "chapters": [
    {
      "id": "figure",
      "title": "Three rotor states side by side",
      "caption": "Three deposited states of Bacillus PS3 ATP synthase, seen from the head; alpha, delta, the peripheral stalk and membrane parts are omitted. Beta sites: amber, closed with Mg-ADP (white); blue, open with putative phosphate (pink, Pi?); green, open and empty. The beta sites change shape around the rotor. Solid red gamma and orange epsilon show the rotor. Gamma differs by about 119 then 121 degrees counterclockwise across panels; angles start at state 1. A different beta site is closed in each state.",
